  "ranked_root_causes": [
    {
      "cause": "offensive_shot_resolution_compression",
      "classification": "HIGH",
      "evidence": "Real team-level shooting-percentage gap between strong/weak primary fives in large/extreme games (three_pt_pct real gap=0.0530, strong 40.4% vs weak 35.1%; two_pt_pct real gap=0.0715, strong 59.2% vs weak 52.1%) collapses in simulated output to three_pt_pct gap=0.00089 (~98% erased) and two_pt_pct gap=0.0294 (~59% erased). This happens even though the underlying per-player scoring-field engine INPUTS are real and correctly directioned (strong team ahead in 75-94% of large/extreme games across every scoring field per category_gap_report). Mean simulated possession count is stable across the bucket (203.7 +/- 9.4), so this is not a pace/volume effect -- the collapse is specifically in shot-make-probability resolution, not shot volume.",
      "rank": 1,
      "superseded_note": "SUPERSEDED by three-point resolution diagnostic (backtests/three_point_resolution_before.json): the ~98%-erased 3PT figure compared single-game realized box-score gaps (noise-dominated) with a simulated gap aggregated over team sets that overlapped (a team can be both strong and weak across games). Per-game paired, the sim gap (0.36pp) tracks its expected make-probability gap (0.54pp); shot_make_probability passes skill through at slope ~0.98."
    },
